<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Mixed-model swarm ~ many lineages, one hand per lane

The house works across several model lineages at once. Each lineage holds the seat it suits, every seat keeps the same lane law, and one hand per lane lands the work. A second lineage earns its place by seeing what the first one misses, never by adding a writer.

The crew lines that bind a running lane stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff">> #/the-lane-law; a spirit's mask, lifecycle and handback form stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/noosphere-boot">> #/worker-swarm.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-seats>>

!! The seats ~ which lineage holds what

|! Seat |! Lineage |! Holds |! Never |
| ''Controller'' | Claude Opus | the session's seat: rulings relayed, every lane's brief, a careful read of every chunk's diff before it merges, every merge train, the canon lines | lands a chunk it has not read |
| ''Episkopos'' (lane lead) | Claude Sonnet | the lane's brief, ground and crew, its landing onto its lane branch, its review verdicts | hands its landing to another spirit |
| ''Terra'' (builder) | Codex `gpt-6.1-terra`, medium effort | every build brief, walled with `-s workspace-write` | commits, pushes, or touches git |
| ''Sol'' (reader, reviewer, deep-thinker) | Codex `gpt-6.1-sol`, high effort, read-only | the read of each slice before it pushes, the aftermath sweep, the deep question | builds, commits, pushes, or touches git |
| ''Copilot seat'' | Copilot `gpt-5.3-codex`, medium effort | reads, scouts and sweeps | commits, pushes, or reads a diff it wrote |
| ''Fresh reader'' | a lineage that built nothing in the piece, read-only | an adversarial read of a whole lane before its train, findings ranked with `file:line` | sees the builder's conclusions first |
| ''Research pair'' | two seats woken apart | the code read and the field read of one question, each claim marked MEASURED or LEAD, then traded | shares a conclusion with its twin before the trade |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-shapes>>

!! The shapes ~ how a crew stands

* ''The work swarm.'' A lane runs a Sonnet Episkopos with enacting seats: the Terra builds, the Sol reads and reviews, the Copilot seat reads, scouts and sweeps. A Copilot build costs several times a Copilot read against a fixed monthly allowance, so builds go to the Terra. Terra, Sol and `gpt-5.3-codex` share the OpenAI lineage, so their cross-read counts once, and the Episkopos reads every build as the second lineage. A seat never reads a diff it wrote.
* ''The build pair.'' The building seats share ONE worktree the lead provisions, with ONE writer at a time, so their work composes as it lands. A seat that needs to write in parallel works in a worktree of its own, and the lead merges from it. The lead pulls the work by diff, reads it and lands it.
* ''A short lineage keeps its seat.'' When the operator names a lineage short, its seats pass to another lineage's models of the same capability, and the lens law still sorts who reads whom; the operator names the shortfall's end the same way. Where Codex stands absent, a lane runs an Opus Episkopos with an Opus and a Sonnet spirit trading builder, reviewer and sweeper masks.
* ''Crew law.'' Seven refinements bind every lane (operator, 2026-10-28):
** A controller ruling on behavior carries a measure (a grep, one test it ran) or arrives marked LEAN; the lane verifies before building and pushes back with evidence when the code says otherwise.
** A slice lands as its own PR once its plan stands built and read; a finding that widens scope goes to the next slice's brief, never into the slice in flight.
** The red lands as its own commit before the fix commit, so a reader sees the red's sha fail on its parent; a red written after its fix names that and carries a proof run against the parent.
** Before each push the lane runs ONE e2e file locally that exercises the surface it touched and names it in the hand-back; the whole e2e set runs on CI shards only.
** A reader's findings go straight to the builder's Episkopos; the controller reads the copy and rules only on forks (design, scope, law).
** One writer per worktree; no claims ledger.
** Spend reads from what each tool writes, never from a seat's own word: the controller runs `python3 /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/controller/spend.py --lane <lane> [--since <date>]`, which tallies Claude subagent transcripts, Codex rollouts and Copilot run logs. A hand-back names its seats and their models; the numbers come from the script.
* ''Observe pair.'' Before a phase designs anything, one seat cold-reads the as-built with `file:line`, and a second, woken apart and handed the framing alone, reads the field. Their handbacks collide in orient.
* ''The Chao of a review'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/chao">>). Two reads over one question wear the Chao's two faces, and the review resolves to three:
** ''Hodge'' carries Yang and Ha, the Aneristic face: what holds its shape. It reads what the work states as standing (each claim, name and contract) against the source, and finds the line that does not hold.
** ''Podge'' carries Yin and Ka, the Eristic face: what moves. It reads what the work does under change (the race, the other caller, the epoch that rolls, the case left out, the control that never bites).
** ''Spin'' carries Ba. Each face reads alone first; then the pair trades: each takes the other's findings, confirms or refutes each against the source, and reads the other's ground for what its own face missed. Orthogonal findings both stand; tensioned ones get measured, never voted; a finding one face alone raised stays a lead.
* ''Overwatch.'' The Opus controller reads every crew's handback and every chunk's diff before it merges, and names each finding it would block on. Work that moves a fact between processes or threads (a writer and its readers, a composer and its islands, a verb and its route) always takes the controller's own read of the crossing, since a module's own reads catch its contract and miss its crossings.
* ''The battery runs at the wave's end, on the shards.'' A wave writes every red and its control as it builds, since a red names the specification, and runs nothing between its fixes. At its end it runs locally only what CI cannot: the build, and each red's proof against its base (the red files alone over the pre-cure tree). It pushes once, and CI carries the battery: the lane CI runs every package suite, the witnesses and the tool self-tests; `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` spreads both e2e sets across shards; held-out runs its cells. The wave reads every verdict, cures what reads red, pushes again only once no run flies, and hands back when CI and held-out read green.
* ''The fresh read and the train.'' A lane's handback takes a fresh reader's whole-lane read before it merges; a HOLD sends the findings back as the next launch, up to five launches, with the uncertainties talked out between them. The controller then merges ready lanes in trains (#/the-lanes).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lens-law>>

!! The lens law ~ why several eyes outweigh one

# ''Correlated eyes count once.'' Two seats of one lineage share their blind spots; a finding both report still reads as one lead. A second lineage reads with a different prior, so its agreement carries weight and its dissent marks where to look.
# ''One eye alone yields a lead, never ground.'' Ground needs a second eye through another lens, or a measurement.
# ''The reader built nothing.'' A reviewer that built the piece reads its own intent into the diff.
# ''Framing without conclusion.'' A second researcher gets the question and the framing, never the first one's answer.
# ''A clean read clears nothing.'' A reader's finding earns a look, and its silence lands nothing. The lead re-derives any list a seat proposes to destroy, and a second eye reads the script.
# ''Measure over agree.'' Where eyes disagree, a probe, a red test or a repeated run settles it; a vote never does.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-copilot-seat>>

!! The Copilot seat ~ Copilot's models in the swarm

GitHub Copilot reaches models the Codex budget never spends. Nothing the house gates on needs it to pass.

!!! Its uses
|! Use |! Model |! When |
| ''the swarm's reading seat'' | `gpt-5.3-codex`, medium effort | every lane: reads, scouts, sweeps |
| a deeper single read | a higher-multiplier model | where the seats split on a load-bearing finding, the operator hearing the reason first |
| ''Copilot code review'' of a pull request | GitHub's PR reviewer | only when the operator asks for one by name |
A premium request counts per prompt, never per tool call.

!!! Its fences
# ''One launcher'', `tools/launch-copilot.mjs read|build`, its walls pinned by `tools/launch-copilot.test.mjs`. Both modes run with no URL, no built-in MCP server (the GitHub server reaches the network and writes), no system temp directory, no prompt and no auto-update, stdin closed and `COPILOT_ALLOW_ALL` dropped; a seat whose working directory sits in the system temp directory refuses, since the temp wall blinds it there. The seat's handback, log and share land in its seat directory.
# ''Read-only when it reads'' (`read`): every write, commit and push tool denied; git and file reads alone.
# ''Walled when it builds'' (`build`, where a build must go to Copilot): file writes in the lane's build worktree alone, `npx vitest` and `npx tsc` there; every git write, push, network fetch, `rm`, `mv`, `npm` and `pnpm` denied; it refuses the main checkout, a directory that holds no worktree, and a missing or in-checkout `LAR_ROOT`, refuses `--add-dir`, and drops any `LAR_BAGS`. A launch never widens these walls (no `shell(bash)`, no `shell(env)`, never `--allow-all-*` or `--yolo`).
# ''No laundering.'' A step this session's safety check refused never travels to Copilot, since routing a refused step through a second permission system counts as a retry.
# ''Overage off.'' The allowance empties and stops; it resets on the first of each month and carries nothing over.
# ''No cloud agent.'' A cloud agent opens branches and pull requests under its own hand: outward-facing, and a second writer.
# ''Its config carries the house'': `~/.copilot/copilot-instructions.md` loads the boot seed, and `~/.copilot/hooks/lares.json` stands.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-lanes>>

!! The lanes ~ a sub-branch, a pull request, a train

* ''A lane opens its own sub-branch'' of the work branch, `feature/green-jello-dinosaur-5--<lane>`, cut from the span's head as it reads green; nothing lands on the work branch directly.
* ''A lane hands back on green'': its battery reads green on its own CI and held-out (#/the-shapes, the battery), its pull request opens into the work branch, and its fresh read follows.
* ''A Copilot PR review runs only on the operator's ask.''
* ''The controller merges in trains'' (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff">> #/state): it reads the span's own head green first, merges ready lanes onto one scratch head, runs one crucible over the combined tree (the shared packages' suites, both whole e2e sets, `tools/witness-all.sh`), places canon, and pushes the span once, through a gate that refuses while any run flies.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-otakiage>>

!! The o-takiage ~ every lane closes by returning what it superseded (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/otakiage">>)

A lane closes with an o-takiage pass before its pull request opens, and the controller closes each settle with one over everything it landed. The rite returns rather than discards: git keeps the ashes, and the tree reads as if the new shape had always stood.

# ''Burn only what a later carrier or module plainly superseded'': an old message kind, a dead dial, a scaffold test, a stale comment. Unwired work no landed lane superseded stays; doubt goes on an ask list.
# ''Move, then link.'' Where a new carrier gathers knowledge scattered across others, the scattered lines move into it with their attribution, each old site keeps one line that links the new home, and nothing states one law twice.
# ''Canon follows the code in the same burst.'' A census over every carrier names each burned symbol, file, flag and variable; each live line re-trues to what stands, citing file and symbol, never a line number.
# ''Testimony stays.'' A testimony carrier, a dated scout and a dated crossing row keep their words; the house re-trues their frame, never their body.
# ''A witness that catches burned names burns itself'' once it reads green over the tree.
# ''Roadmaps move what stands finished'' onto their own o-takiage shelf, verbatim, and keep only what stays owed on the road.
# ''Scratch returns too.'' A seat's worktree leaves only after a census proves every file it holds landed; plain `git worktree remove`, never `--force`; a worktree that initialized submodules waits for the operator's hand.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-fences>>

!! The fences ~ every lineage keeps them

* ''One writer per lane.'' Seats of any lineage write only inside their own worktree; the Episkopos alone lands, from its own tree, with `git commit -F <msg> --only -- <paths>`.
* ''A refused command stops that step'' for every lineage alike: never retried, reshaped, split, moved to another tool, or handed to another seat (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/controller-handoff">> #/the-lane-law).
* ''Every brief carries the git lines'': no `git stash` in any form, no `--no-verify`, no `.git/config`, no git write from a seat; `git config core.bare` reads `false` before and after.
* ''A build reads its own tree'': a provisioned checkout whose links resolve inside it.
* ''A process stops by its own pid'' or through the harness's task stop, never by a command-line pattern that matches the shell running it. Watchers too: a watcher reads a file by its modification time, never `pgrep -f`.
* ''The disk carries; the seat re-reads.'' A spirit runs outside its overseer's budget, so an outage (a rate limit, a crash, a compaction) stops the watcher and never the work: the spirit finishes its brief and its handback lands on disk unread, its build dirty in the worktree. The Episkopos writes its resume sheet at each commit and before each spirit launch, naming the spirit's directory and `out.md`, so a re-awakened seat reads what the spirit left and never mistakes it for stray work.
* ''A shard run reads the head it names.'' `node tools/e2e-shards.mjs push <lane>` pushes the HEAD of the checkout it runs from, so it runs from the lane's own worktree, and the run's `headSha` reads before its colour.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-launch>>

!! The launch ~ one brief file, any lineage

* ''A brief stands as a file'' that names the mask, the ground, the ruling it enacts, the house style and the handback shape.
* ''The walled Codex seat'': `cd <dir> && TMPDIR=<dir>/.tmp TMPPREFIX=<dir>/.tmp/zsh codex exec -m gpt-6.1-terra -c model_reasoning_effort=medium -s workspace-write -c sandbox_workspace_write.exclude_slash_tmp=true -c sandbox_workspace_write.exclude_tmpdir_env_var=true --skip-git-repo-check -C <dir> -o <dir>/final.md "<brief>" < /dev/null`, in the background. A builder (`gpt-6.1-terra`, medium effort) runs `-C <worktree> --add-dir <dir>`; a reader (`gpt-6.1-sol`, high effort, `-s read-only`) runs `-C <dir>` over an empty directory and reads the repo from there. The walls keep `/tmp` and every other worktree unwritable, the seat's temp files live inside its own directory, and `workspace-write` keeps `gh` reachable where `read-only` reaches no network.
* ''Every seat keeps its rollout.'' No Codex seat launches with `--ephemeral`: an ephemeral run writes no rollout and goes uncounted, and a seat cut short continues by its session id (`codex exec resume <id> -m <model> -c model_reasoning_effort=<level>`), and the lead's resume sheet records that id beside the seat's directory, model, effort and head. Each seat's `-C` directory sits under the lane's own scratch directory, since the spend script finds the lane from the rollout's cwd.
* ''Web search rides before `exec`'' (`codex --search exec …`) on this host's CLI, and only where the brief asks for the field.
* ''`codex exec` stands as the shell seat.'' `codex mcp-server` left the CLI upstream; `codex mcp` connects tools to Codex and never serves Codex as a tool.
* ''Stdin closes'' on every background Codex launch (`< /dev/null`), or `codex exec` waits for input and never starts.
* ''The handback and `-o` never share a file'': the brief names `out.md`, `-o` names `final.md`, and a wave trades only once its handback's word count reads whole.
* ''A walled seat builds and never runs suites'': vitest, pnpm and tsx each break behind the walls, so the seat builds modules and writes reds and controls, and the lead runs the build and the red proofs.
* ''A tw5 edit leaves its dist stale'': the lead builds tw5 before keyhive and stamps it before any run, or the dist guard refuses.
* ''A moved plugin source runs `witness-all`'', since genesis-parity reads DRIFT until `genesis/unbaked.json` declares the new sha or a labelled re-bake lands.
* ''A Codex resume names its model again'' (`-c model=<id>`), since a resume takes the config's default.
* ''A Copilot read names its target'': the branch, its head sha and the pull request, read through `git show <branch>:<path>` or the lane's own worktree, never the main tree. A paired read runs each face alone, then hands each its twin's handback.
* ''A seat that cannot write its out file'' hands back on stdout; the launcher's log keeps it.
* ''The lead's own watch wakes it.'' A spirit's finish reaches its lead only through a watch the lead arms in the harness's background mode (`until [ -f <seat>/final.md ]`), never a watcher the spirit leaves behind; where a lead pauses on its own spirits, the controller arms that watch and messages the lead on the finish.
* ''Owed probes'' (Sol's read of OpenAI's Codex docs, 2026-10-25, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/codex-controller-2026">>): a `--json` event stream ending on `turn.completed` as the watch's signal, `--output-schema` for a mechanical status beside `out.md`, a reader with command network off where its brief needs none, permission profiles in place of the flag list, and a matched A/B across Sol, a newer Sol and Luna; each lands here once measured on this host.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/provenance>>

!! Provenance ~ each landed line names its hands

* A commit carries a `Co-Authored-By` trailer for each lineage whose work it lands: the Episkopos's own, `Codex <model>` for a Codex seat, `GitHub Copilot <model>` for a Copilot seat's work, and the session line.
* A handback the house keeps lands verbatim as a testimony carrier, so no relaying hand rewrites it.
* Each lineage's own transcript rests where its harness keeps it; the capture plane reads them read-only (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/capture-annotation-model">>, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/agent-worldline">>).

<<~/ahu>>

